    "text": "> At the server site, a server asks for incoming and outgoing queues, using its well-known port, when it starts running. The queues remain open as long as the server is running. > When a message arrives for server, UDP sends received user datagram to end of incoming queue. > If there is no such queue, or incoming queue overflow, UDP discards the user datagram and asks the ICMP protocol to send a port unreachable message to the client. > When a server wants to respond to a client, it sends messages to the outgoing queue, using the source port number specified in the request. | BRR > UDP removes the messages one by one and, after adding the UDP header, delivers them to IP. > If outgoing queue overflows, OS asks the server to wait before sending any more messages.",
